---
/**
 * Escapes the prose column out to the full width of the content panel, for a
 * figure or video the design runs wider than the text.
 *
 * How far it may reach is the layout's business rather than the component's,
 * so `ArticleLayout` states it as `--breakout-start` / `--breakout-end`. Two
 * values, not one: the contents list takes the end side of the card at desktop
 * widths, which leaves the prose column sitting off-center in it. Used
 * anywhere that says nothing about its room — a `Note`'s inner prose, say —
 * both fall back to no escape at all.
 *
 * Belongs at the top level of an article's prose. In a list or a table cell
 * the margins would still apply, and would tear the content out of the thing
 * indenting it.
 *
 * Content keeps its own width: an image narrower than the panel is centered
 * rather than stretched. Nothing here carries `prose-end`, so a `figcaption`
 * is still styled as prose — put it on a wrapper inside if a subtree should
 * opt out.
 */
---

<div class="breakout">
  <slot />
</div>

<style>
  .breakout {
    margin-inline: calc(-1 * var(--breakout-start, 0rem))
      calc(-1 * var(--breakout-end, 0rem));
  }

  /*
   * Slotted content is rendered by the file that wrote it, so it carries that
   * file's scope hash and not this component's — `:global` is what lets the
   * rule reach it at all.
   *
   * An auto inline margin centers a replaced element narrower than the panel
   * and does nothing to one that fills it, which is why this is narrower than
   * a rule on every child: a list's own indent would lose to it.
   */
  .breakout :global(:is(img, picture, svg, video, iframe)) {
    margin-inline: auto;
  }
</style>
